WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.960
Hello everyone, and welcome to the Langfocus channel.
My name is Paul.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.020
Today I am going to talk about the Spanish language.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.040
As you may know, Spain has more than one language,

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.400
so when I say "Spanish," I'm referring specifically to 
Castilian Spanish, which is spoken as a native language

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.020
by seventy-four percent of the people of Spain

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.140
and ninety-nine percent of the people of Spain,
including second language speakers.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.600
Castilian is the Spanish language that grew to become 
one of the most important global languages.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.980
During this video, I might refer to it as Castilian, 
to contrast it with other languages of Spain,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.660
but usually I'll refer to it as Spanish.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.500
Out of all languages in the world, Spanish has 
the second largest number of native speakers,

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:53.600
with 470 million native speakers, as well as another 
90 million who speak it as a second language.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.540
It is also one of the most widely dispersed languages,

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:01.640
with 44 countries of the world having at least 
three million native speakers of Spanish or more.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:07.440
It is one of the six official languages of the UN, as well as an official language of the European Union.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.660
It is also an official language of 21 countries, if we include Puerto Rico, which is a territory of the US.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.680
It also used to be an official language
of the Philippines, but it is not anymore.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.820
Spanish is also the third most widely used language on the Internet. According to one source that I consulted:

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:29.140
"The use of Spanish online has grown
 by 800% in the past few years."

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:34.220
Spanish is a member of the Ibero-Romance sub-family 
of the Romance language family.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.400
Other members of that sub-family include Portuguese, Galician, and some people say, Catalan.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:46.740
Like all Romance languages, Spanish developed 
from Vulgar Latin, the spoken Latin that was brought 
to the area by the Roman Empire.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:53.640
Vulgar Latin evolved into numerous regional dialects, which eventually became distinct enough 
to be considered different languages.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:58.380
There are documents from the ninth century that are written in an early form of Castilian Spanish,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.340
or we could say a late form of Vulgar Latin.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:07.340
In any case, documents like these show 
that the Vulgar Latin dialects were becoming
separate languages by that point.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:13.500
Castilian Spanish is the language that arose in the north 
central region of the Iberian Peninsula, called Castille

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.900
and it grew more and more distinct
 from its neighbor language, Leonese.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:22.720
It became firmly established as a distinct language
in the thirteenth century when King Alfonso decided

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.040
to standardize the written language for official use,
based on the dialect spoken in Toledo.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.280
A process which continued for a few hundred years
until the sixteenth century.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.780
In the 1300s, the southern part of the Iberian Peninsula
was under the control of Al-Andalus,

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.940
a Muslim-controlled territory that had previously 
controlled most of the peninsula.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.580
The Reconquista, which means "The Reconquest,"

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:50.200
resulted in the retaking of the Muslim areas and
of the spread of Castilian Spanish to the south.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.640
There, it was influenced by the Mozarabic dialects.
Those were Romance dialects,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.160
spoken by Muslims in Al-Andalus,
that contained a lot of loanwords from Arabic.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.380
Castilian Spanish absorbed a lot of
those Arabic loan words,

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.740
up to four thousand words, 
or eight percent of the modern language's vocabulary.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:11.380
Some common Spanish words of Arabic origin are: 
* "hasta," which means "until."

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.960
That comes from the Arabic word "hatta," 
with the same meaning;

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:20.639
"ojalá," which means "I hope," comes from
the Arabic word "law sha'allah," or "in sha'allah,"

00:03:20.639 --> 00:03:22.439
which means "if it's God's will";

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:28.259
"aceite," which means "oil," as in olive oil, 
comes from the Arabic word "az-zayt,"
with the same meaning.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.960
There are also a lot of words in Spanish that begin 
with "A-L," "al," which is the Arabic definite article.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:39.520
For example, "alcohol," from the Arabic word "al-kuHuul," 
which, of course, means "alcohol";

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:46.080
and "alcalde," which means "mayor," comes from 
the Arabic word "al-qaa-Dii," which means "judge" in Arabic.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.420
That's just a couple examples, but there
are lots of words beginning with "al" in Spanish.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:56.000
The Christian reconquest of the south successfully 
ended in 1492, and that very same year,

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.660
Spain began sending explorers, like Christopher Columbus,
abroad to build the Spanish Empire.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.320
The Spanish language spread, along
 with the empire, to the Americas,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.180
to the Spanish East Indies, which includes present-day 
Philippines, and to some parts of Africa.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.760
One area it spread to was Mexico, which today has 
the largest Spanish-speaking population of any country,

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.239
with120 million speakers of Spanish.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.700
The variety of Spanish spoken in Mexico varies
somewhat from the variety spoken in Spain,

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.620
and that's true for the other
Spanish-speaking countries as well.

00:04:25.789 --> 00:04:28.649
The standard written language is
basically the same everywhere,

00:04:28.649 --> 00:04:33.620
but the spoken language has some differences,
especially in pronunciation and in vocabulary,

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.440
and especially in common daily vocabulary, 
like clothing, food, and things like that.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:43.620
In Latin America, various native languages influence 
the different local varieties of Spanish.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.860
What's the best variety of Spanish to learn?

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:51.080
Well, the varieties are often categorized into 
European Spanish and Latin American Spanish.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.780
From what I understand, 
there isn't a lot of difference for beginners.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:59.680
No matter which variety you learn, you will be able 
to adapt to other varieties quite easily.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.020
Personally, I've met more Spanish speakers 
from Mexico than from any other country,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:11.600
so for me that's the natural choice, but for you, 
it depends on which variety you are most likely to use
and which one you're most interested in.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:16.159
Like Romance languages in general, 
Spanish shares a lot of vocabulary with English --

00:05:16.159 --> 00:05:20.899
not identical vocabulary, 
but related vocabulary that you can figure out

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.380
and you can more easily remember 
if you associate it with the related English words.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.400
Let's take a look at a couple sentences:
"¿Cuánto cuesta un viaje a Nueva York?"

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:34.180
Okay, so the first word, "cuánto," 
that sounds to me like "quantity,"

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.160
so I think it means "how much,"
because this is a question.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.500
The next word, "cuesta," 
well, I think that probably means "cost."

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.970
The next word "un", 
I know that means "a", the indefinite article.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.280
The next word "viaje". Well, that looks like "voyage" to me, 
or "voyage" in French,

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.000
so I think it means a "voyage" or a "trip,"  
and then "a," I know means "to,"

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.540
and "Nueva York", of course, that means "New York".

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:00.060
So I think this means :
"How much does a trip to New York cost?"

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:04.420
If we translate it literally, 
"How much cost a trip to New York?"

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:09.199
Another sentence: 
"El hombre conduce un automóvil."

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:14.280
So let's look at it word by word: 
"El," I know, that means "the," the definite article,

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:18.680
"Hombre," that's a word I know from movies and stuff;
I think that means "man."

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:22.520
"Conduce," well, I'm not sure. 
Let's come back to that one in a second.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:26.640
"Un" is "a" 
and "automóvil" that means "automobile" = "car."

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.740
So go back to "conduce," 
well maybe that means "conduct".

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:34.819
or in this case, you don't conduct an automobile, 
you drive an automobile.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:38.860
So this means "The man drives an automobile" 
or "The man drives a car."

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.760
So you can see that you can figure out what those words 
mean if you look at each word and analyze them.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.300
It's not always that easy, but that's an 
easy example, so you know what I mean.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.740
And also, if we look at the syntax of those sentences, 
we can see that they're very similar to English.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:59.000
So the first sentence again, 
"¿Cuánto cuesta un viaje un Neuva York?"
"How much cost a trip to New York?"

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.320
Quite similar to English, but just sort of simplified.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.680
The next sentence: "The man drives a car."

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:11.020
That one is word-by-word exactly the same as English, 
so it's translatable directly.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:16.580
Of course, the grammar gets more complicated than that, 
but just the most basic syntax  is very similar to English.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:21.199
So this is one of the benefits of learning 
a Romance language for English speakers.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.700
According to the American Foreign Service Institute, Spanish is a Category I language.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.800
That means that, relatively speaking, it is one of the 
easier languages for native English speakers to learn.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.440
So what are you waiting for?
Learn some español, or should I say, castellano?

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.760
I'm not sure, because Spanish speakers
themselves can't actually agree on this issue.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:42.780
In the red-coloured countries, 
the preferred term is "castellano,"

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.020
and in the blue-coloured countries, 
the preferred term is "español."

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:50.060
And in Spain, you can see that there is some
disagreement about which term is best.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:55.900
If you're a native speaker of Spanish,
maybe you can tell us what you think
about that in the comments down below.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.400
And other people, you can leave your comments, too.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:01.240
Thank you for watching. Have a nice day.

